        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How do I keep my child safe around a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Keep children out of the room while the treadmill is running, remove the safety key and unplug the machine after every use, store the key out of reach, and never let children play on the machine, even switched off. The US Consumer Product Safety Commission has warned that children can be seriously injured by home treadmills, including being pulled under the rear of the machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the safety key on a treadmill for?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It stops the belt if you fall or drift too far back, because the cord clipped to your clothing pulls the key out of the console. It also prevents the machine from starting without it, which is why you should remove it after each session and keep it away from children. It only works if it is clipped to you every time.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space do you need behind a treadmill for safety?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Plan for roughly 6 feet of clear floor behind the rear of the belt and about 2 feet on each side; some manuals specify around 6.5 feet, and your manual’s figure should be followed. The zone should be free of walls, furniture, glass doors, stairs and other equipment.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it safe to have a treadmill with pets?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, with precautions. Keep pets out of the room or away from the rear and sides while the belt is running, never leave a pet alone with a running machine, check under the deck and inside a folded unit before use, and vacuum pet hair regularly. Ask a vet before trying to exercise a dog on a treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you plug a treadmill into an extension cord?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most manufacturers advise against it and specify plugging directly into a grounded outlet, and some larger machines require a dedicated circuit. An extension cord or power strip not rated for the draw can overheat and is also a trip hazard. Follow your manual.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it safe to wear headphones on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Generally yes, with care. Wireless headphones avoid cords catching on handrails, and keeping the volume low enough to hear your name means you will notice if a child or pet enters the room. Avoid turning to watch a screen to the side, which makes most people drift.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

